From: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 03:44:13 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] screencast: the stream of a networked output is that output's picture

A network screen in one of its 3D formats streams what that format sends: each eye drawn at the device
raster, squeezed into its half for side by side half and top and bottom, mixed with the Dubois
matrices for the anaglyph twins, the eyes in the order the output's swap gives; in 2D one view, as before.
The matrices move into opengl/anaglyphmatrix.h, which the DRM layer now uses too: one copy. Rows, columns
and checkerboard are drawn with the shader the screen's own layer uses (opengl/stereopattern.frag), the first
eye on the first row, column or pixel the receiver sees; side by side (full) places each eye whole in its half.
A format or eye swap chosen while the screen streams repaints the whole picture and sends a frame (it
damages nothing in the scene). The typed panel request accepts a network screen that comes back in the
3D format saved for its device: it compares the split-eye flag with the panel's own and asks for eyes
only of a split-eye panel, the headset, as before.

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5
